  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="Tag254" contextRef="C1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="text-decoration: underline; font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note 1 - Organization and Nature of Operations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;High Island Offshore System, L.L.C. (HIOS or the Company), a Delaware limited liability company, is a wholly-owned, indirect subsidiary of Genesis Energy, L.P. (Genesis), a publicly traded master limited partnership. Effective July 24, 2015, HIOS was acquired by Genesis from Enterprise Products Partners L.P. (EPD), a publicly traded master limited partnership.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;The Company owns an undersea natural gas gathering and transmission system, which includes the East Breaks Gathering System and three supply legs that connect to a 42-inch diameter mainline. The Company gathers and transports natural gas received from fields located in the Galveston, Garden Banks, High Island, West Cameron and East Breaks areas of the Gulf of America to a junction platform owned by the Company located in West Cameron Block 167. The Company provides gathering and transportation services as authorized by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC). The Company's major customers include natural gas marketers and producers.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Note 2 - Basis of Presentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;These financial statements were prepared in conformity with the accounting requirements of the FERC Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States. The most significant differences between accounting principles generally accepted in the United States and the financial statements presented herein are; (1) FERC does not permit "push down" accounting for a purchase acquisition, (2) the presentation of removal costs collected from customers, and (3) the classification of certain accounts on the balance sheet and statements of income.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;These financial statements should be read along with the Company&amp;#8217;s audited financial statements and notes thereto filed on FERC Form No. 2 for the year ended December 31, 2025, which&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;includes a summary of the Company&amp;#8217;s significant accounting policies and other disclosures. The financial statements as of March 31, 2026 and the results of operations and cash flows for the three&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;months ended March 31, 2026 and 2025 are unaudited. The Company derived the balance sheet as of March 31, 2026, from the audited balance sheet included in the Company&amp;#8217;s FERC Form No.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;2 for the year ended December 31, 2025. The Company, in its opinion, has made all adjustments, all of which are of a normal, recurring nature, to fairly present its interim results. Information for&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;interim periods may not depict the results of operations for the entire year.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;In preparing these financial statements, the Company has evaluated subsequent events for potential recognition or disclosure through May 26, 2026.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Note 3 - Regulatory Matters&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;The Company is subject to the jurisdiction of the FERC in accordance with the Natural Gas Act of 1938 and the Natural Gas Policy Act of 1978. The Company operates certain natural gas pipelines under a FERC approved tariff.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;On March 10, 2026, HIOS filed to conclude the Storm Damage Surcharge that was effective April 1, 2021, pursuant to the terms stipulated in its tariff.&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;Under Section 18.5(d) of the GT&amp;amp;C, if the amount in the Storm Damage Surcharge account is less than $100,000 (either negative or positive), HIOS is permitted to reduce the Storm Damage Surcharge to $0.00/Dth and to refund or direct bill shippers the remaining balance. Such refunds or direct bills are required to be based pro rata on the actual quantities transported by HIOS for the applicable month.&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 18.6667px;"&gt;The remaining balance was collected in the second quarter of 2026.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;strong style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Note 4 - Contingencies&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;Certain conditions may exist as of the date the financial statements are issued, which may result in a loss to the Company but which will only be resolved when one or more future events occur or fail to occur. The Company's management and its legal counsel assess such contingent liabilities, and such assessment inherently involves an exercise of judgment. In assessing loss contingencies related to legal proceedings that are pending against the Company or un-asserted claims that may result in such proceedings, the Company's legal counsel evaluates the perceived merits of any legal proceedings or un-asserted claims as well as the perceived merits of the amount of relief sought or expected to be sought therein.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;If the assessment of a contingency indicates that it is probable that a material loss has been incurred and the amount of the liability can be estimated, then the estimated liability would be accrued and the nature of the contingent liability would be disclosed in the Company's financial statements.If the assessment indicates that a potentially material loss contingency is not probable, but is reasonably possible, or is probable but cannot be estimated, then the nature of the contingent liability, together with an estimate of the range of possible loss if determinable and material, would be disclosed.Loss contingencies considered remote are generally not disclosed or recorded unless they involve guarantees that are material to the Company, in which case the nature of the guarantee would be disclosed. The Company had no matters requiring loss contingency accruals at March 31, 2026.&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
